WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:04.790
To plan dives and reserves you need one number: a key part of gas planning.

00:05.890 --> 00:11.350
Your surface consumption rate, SCR: the litres you breathe in a minute at the surface.

00:11.350 --> 00:18.020
Also called SAC, surface air consumption, but the gas is not always air: nitrox, trimix.

00:20.120 --> 00:29.090
An example dive: twenty metres average depth, thirty-five minutes, an eleven point one litre aluminium tank, two hundred bar down to sixty.

00:30.840 --> 00:33.520
Step one: two hundred down to sixty bar.

00:33.640 --> 00:34.970
A hundred and forty bar used.

00:36.420 --> 00:40.780
Step two: a hundred and forty bar times eleven point one litres.

00:40.960 --> 00:42.710
Fifteen hundred and fifty-four litres.

00:45.010 --> 00:48.030
Step three: over thirty-five minutes.

00:48.550 --> 00:50.990
Forty-four point four litres a minute at depth.

00:52.430 --> 00:56.070
Step four: twenty metres is twenty over ten, plus one.

00:56.110 --> 00:57.420
Three atmospheres.

00:58.670 --> 01:00.910
Step five: divide by that pressure.

01:01.010 --> 01:04.690
Fourteen point eight, rounded up to fifteen litres a minute at the surface.

01:04.690 --> 01:06.780
That is the rate for this dive.

01:09.880 --> 01:14.620
Over several dives, easy and hard, plan with the higher figure, rounded up.

01:15.600 --> 01:20.960
For an easy rule, plan with twenty litres a minute: in bar, every five minutes.

01:21.040 --> 01:21.820
Breathe more?

01:21.820 --> 01:23.420
Use your own number.

01:23.460 --> 01:24.320
Breathe less?

01:24.440 --> 01:25.170
Extra margin.

01:28.270 --> 01:30.470
Some computers track it with a transmitter;

01:30.570 --> 01:35.670
for Shearwater divers, Dive log shows it for each dive, and soon over time.

01:35.890 --> 01:37.590
Free on the App Store.

01:37.590 --> 01:39.440
diver dot today.
